WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.470
Mutual TLS, often called mTLS, is a pattern where both sides of a
connection present certificates.

2
00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:10.743
A client validates the server identity, and the server also validates the
client certificate.

3
00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:19.641
This can strengthen service identity in service-to-service communication,
device communication, workload identity, or controlled partner
integrations.

4
00:00:19.641 --> 00:00:24.979
It is most useful when both ends are managed and certificate lifecycle can
be operated reliably.

5
00:00:24.979 --> 00:00:27.879
Client certificates require their own trust model.

6
00:00:27.879 --> 00:00:38.029
The server needs a trust store or validation policy for client
certificates, and the application needs a way to map a validated
certificate to an application identity or service role.

7
00:00:38.029 --> 00:00:39.875
That mapping should be explicit.

8
00:00:39.875 --> 00:00:49.234
A certificate may establish that a workload or device is known, but the
application still needs to decide which resources and actions that
identity is authorized to use.

9
00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:54.968
mTLS can improve identity assurance, but it does not automatically
authorize every requested action.

10
00:00:54.968 --> 00:00:56.747
Least privilege still matters.

11
00:00:56.747 --> 00:01:05.777
A service with a valid client certificate should not necessarily call
every internal endpoint, read every tenant record, or perform
administrative operations.

12
00:01:05.777 --> 00:01:14.872
Application authorization, request validation, auditing, and monitoring
remain necessary even when the transport layer establishes strong service
identity.

13
00:01:14.872 --> 00:01:16.718
Operational complexity is real.

14
00:01:16.718 --> 00:01:25.747
Certificates expire, keys need protection, trust stores change, clients
are provisioned and deprovisioned, and incidents may require revocation or
rotation.

15
00:01:25.747 --> 00:01:31.877
mTLS should be used with clear ownership, documented renewal processes,
monitoring, and incident response.

16
00:01:31.877 --> 00:01:35.040
When operated well, it can strengthen service identity.

17
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.720
When operated casually, it can create fragile dependencies and confusing
outages.
